1
00:00:00,030 --> 00:00:01,791
It's September nineteen twenty-nine.

2
00:00:02,171 --> 00:00:03,677
You have one thousand dollars.

3
00:00:04,057 --> 00:00:07,825
Three doors: the stock market,
a bank, or a shoebox under your bed.

4
00:00:08,205 --> 00:00:11,000
By nineteen thirty-three,
one door is a disaster,

5
00:00:11,280 --> 00:00:13,766
one is a gamble, and one is a surprise.

6
00:00:14,146 --> 00:00:14,946
Let's play.

7
00:00:15,621 --> 00:00:17,271
I'm Leo, and tonight I'm your host.

8
00:00:17,651 --> 00:00:21,175
I'm a cartoon, so nobody in
this studio loses real money.

9
00:00:21,555 --> 00:00:23,436
The people of nineteen twenty-nine did.

10
00:00:23,816 --> 00:00:27,772
Their question was simple: where was
money safe in nineteen twenty-nine?

11
00:00:28,252 --> 00:00:31,100
By the end, you'll know
which door won, why it won,

12
00:00:31,340 --> 00:00:34,132
and why the same math
runs the other way today.

13
00:00:34,862 --> 00:00:36,840
Here's the idea this game will test:

14
00:00:37,220 --> 00:00:39,732
a safe place for money
isn't really a place.

15
00:00:40,112 --> 00:00:41,909
It depends on what prices are doing.

16
00:00:42,289 --> 00:00:44,189
Now, how big is this thousand dollars?

17
00:00:44,669 --> 00:00:48,180
In nineteen twenty-nine,
an average full-time worker earned

18
00:00:48,180 --> 00:00:51,601
about one thousand four
hundred thirty dollars a year.

19
00:00:51,981 --> 00:00:53,798
So this is roughly eight months of pay.

20
00:00:54,178 --> 00:00:55,258
You don't want to lose it.

21
00:00:55,988 --> 00:00:56,914
Here are the rules.

22
00:00:57,294 --> 00:00:58,462
You can't switch doors.

23
00:00:58,842 --> 00:01:00,096
You can't see the future.

24
00:01:00,476 --> 00:01:01,649
Each door gets a player.

25
00:01:02,029 --> 00:01:03,572
Stock Leo takes the blue door.

26
00:01:03,952 --> 00:01:05,508
Bank Leo takes the green door.

27
00:01:05,888 --> 00:01:09,488
And Box Leo takes the brown one,
holding an actual shoebox.

28
00:01:09,868 --> 00:01:11,987
The studio audience laughs at Box Leo.

29
00:01:12,367 --> 00:01:13,450
Remember that laugh.

30
00:01:13,830 --> 00:01:16,340
Door one goes first, and
in nineteen twenty-nine,

31
00:01:16,340 --> 00:01:19,007
it looks like the smartest
choice in the room.

32
00:01:19,737 --> 00:01:21,663
To see why, look at the last eight years.

33
00:01:22,043 --> 00:01:23,440
In August nineteen twenty-one,

34
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:27,363
the Dow Jones Industrial
Average stood at sixty-three.

35
00:01:27,743 --> 00:01:31,691
By September nineteen twenty-nine,
it was at three hundred eighty-one.

36
00:01:32,071 --> 00:01:33,327
That's six times higher.

37
00:01:33,707 --> 00:01:37,403
Ordinary people, not just bankers,
were putting their savings in.

38
00:01:37,783 --> 00:01:39,587
Many didn't even pay the full price.

39
00:01:39,967 --> 00:01:41,220
They put down part of it,

40
00:01:41,400 --> 00:01:44,674
sometimes as little as ten
percent, and borrowed the rest.

41
00:01:45,054 --> 00:01:47,897
Stock Leo walks through the
blue door on September third.

42
00:01:48,277 --> 00:01:52,110
That day, the Dow closes at three
hundred eighty-one point one seven.

43
00:01:52,490 --> 00:01:53,307
It's the top.

44
00:01:53,687 --> 00:01:54,848
Nobody knows it yet.

45
00:01:55,578 --> 00:02:00,324
For a few weeks, prices swing up and down,
with sudden drops and quick recoveries.

46
00:02:00,704 --> 00:02:01,831
Then October arrives.

47
00:02:02,211 --> 00:02:03,940
On Monday, October twenty-eighth,

48
00:02:04,260 --> 00:02:07,601
the Dow falls nearly thirteen
percent in a single day.

49
00:02:07,981 --> 00:02:10,638
On Tuesday, it falls
almost twelve percent more.

50
00:02:11,018 --> 00:02:13,720
People will call them Black
Monday and Black Tuesday.

51
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:15,545
The borrowed money makes it worse.

52
00:02:15,925 --> 00:02:18,800
When prices drop, lenders
want their loans covered,

53
00:02:18,800 --> 00:02:21,438
so people sell, and prices drop again.

54
00:02:21,818 --> 00:02:25,145
By mid-November, the Dow has
lost almost half its value.

55
00:02:25,525 --> 00:02:28,960
Stock Leo's one thousand dollars
is now about five hundred.

56
00:02:29,690 --> 00:02:31,158
Half gone in about ten weeks.

57
00:02:31,538 --> 00:02:32,338
That hurts.

58
00:02:32,654 --> 00:02:34,063
But Stock Leo stays calm.

59
00:02:34,443 --> 00:02:36,874
He thinks this is a dip,
and dips come back.

60
00:02:37,254 --> 00:02:39,888
Over at the green door,
Bank Leo is even calmer.

61
00:02:40,268 --> 00:02:43,434
His money sits in a bank,
with a vault and a marble floor.

62
00:02:43,814 --> 00:02:44,900
That's the safe door.

63
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:46,080
Right?

64
00:02:46,532 --> 00:02:48,772
Here's the part that
surprises people today.

65
00:02:49,152 --> 00:02:54,608
In nineteen twenty-nine, a bank account in
America had no federal deposit insurance.

66
00:02:54,988 --> 00:02:58,313
If your bank closed, nobody
promised you'd get your money back.

67
00:02:58,693 --> 00:02:59,946
And there were a lot of banks.

68
00:03:00,326 --> 00:03:01,340
When the trouble began,

69
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:05,420
over eight thousand banks belonged
to the Federal Reserve System,

70
00:03:05,420 --> 00:03:08,100
and nearly sixteen thousand did not.

71
00:03:08,480 --> 00:03:09,755
Many were small and local.

72
00:03:10,135 --> 00:03:11,747
For a year, Bank Leo is fine.

73
00:03:12,127 --> 00:03:15,458
Then, in late nineteen thirty,
banks start to fail in waves.

74
00:03:15,838 --> 00:03:18,367
Within a few weeks,
hundreds close their doors.

75
00:03:19,097 --> 00:03:23,720
On December eleventh, nineteen thirty,
the fourth-largest bank in New York City,

76
00:03:23,940 --> 00:03:26,670
the Bank of United States,
stops operating.

77
00:03:27,050 --> 00:03:28,350
Picture the street outside.

78
00:03:28,730 --> 00:03:32,424
A long line of people, all asking
for their money at the same time.

79
00:03:32,804 --> 00:03:33,758
Here's the problem.

80
00:03:34,138 --> 00:03:37,847
A bank doesn't keep your thousand
dollars in a box with your name on it.

81
00:03:38,227 --> 00:03:39,455
It lends most of it out.

82
00:03:39,835 --> 00:03:44,215
So when everyone comes at once, even
a healthy bank can run out of cash.

83
00:03:44,595 --> 00:03:47,760
And when one bank falls, people
at the next bank get nervous,

84
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:49,418
and the line moves there.

85
00:03:50,148 --> 00:03:51,456
Now the score for door two.

86
00:03:51,836 --> 00:03:52,938
It isn't one number.

87
00:03:53,318 --> 00:03:54,118
It's two.

88
00:03:54,371 --> 00:03:56,680
From nineteen thirty to
nineteen thirty-three,

89
00:03:57,120 --> 00:04:00,147
about nine thousand banks
suspended operations.

90
00:04:00,527 --> 00:04:02,248
That's more than one bank in three.

91
00:04:02,628 --> 00:04:03,740
Some reopened later.

92
00:04:04,120 --> 00:04:05,293
Thousands never did.

93
00:04:05,673 --> 00:04:09,540
If your bank was one of them,
the FDIC's own numbers show

94
00:04:09,540 --> 00:04:13,870
depositors lost about twenty
cents of every dollar, on average.

95
00:04:14,250 --> 00:04:17,120
For Bank Leo, that's around
two hundred dollars gone,

96
00:04:17,460 --> 00:04:19,762
and he might wait a
long time for the rest.

97
00:04:20,142 --> 00:04:22,289
If your bank survived,
you kept everything.

98
00:04:22,669 --> 00:04:24,325
That's why door two is the gamble.

99
00:04:24,705 --> 00:04:28,650
It all depends on which bank you
picked, and nobody could see inside.

100
00:04:29,380 --> 00:04:30,180
Quick pause.

101
00:04:30,526 --> 00:04:35,276
If you like history played as a game,
subscribing helps Leo build the next one.

102
00:04:35,656 --> 00:04:36,981
Now, back to the studio.

103
00:04:37,361 --> 00:04:38,662
One door is still closed.

104
00:04:39,042 --> 00:04:39,842
Box Leo.

105
00:04:40,159 --> 00:04:41,533
The one everybody laughed at.

106
00:04:41,913 --> 00:04:43,509
He hasn't touched his money once.

107
00:04:43,889 --> 00:04:45,773
And something strange is happening to it.

108
00:04:46,503 --> 00:04:48,866
Box Leo's thousand dollars never changes.

109
00:04:49,246 --> 00:04:51,057
Same stack of bills, every morning.

110
00:04:51,437 --> 00:04:53,477
But the world around the box does change.

111
00:04:53,857 --> 00:04:54,923
Prices are falling.

112
00:04:55,303 --> 00:04:56,695
Not just a few prices.

113
00:04:57,075 --> 00:04:59,088
The average price of what families buy.

114
00:04:59,468 --> 00:05:01,140
The official price index shows it.

115
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:05,040
From September nineteen twenty-nine
to July nineteen thirty-two,

116
00:05:05,540 --> 00:05:08,675
consumer prices fell by
about twenty-one percent.

117
00:05:09,055 --> 00:05:12,707
So the same thousand dollars
now buys what about one thousand

118
00:05:12,707 --> 00:05:16,102
two hundred seventy dollars
bought in nineteen twenty-nine.

119
00:05:16,482 --> 00:05:19,181
Box Leo did nothing,
and his money got stronger.

120
00:05:19,911 --> 00:05:21,119
Why were prices falling?

121
00:05:21,599 --> 00:05:23,167
Fewer jobs meant less spending.

122
00:05:23,547 --> 00:05:25,100
And money itself was shrinking.

123
00:05:25,480 --> 00:05:30,240
Federal Reserve History says the money
supply fell by nearly thirty percent

124
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:34,551
from the fall of nineteen thirty to
the winter of nineteen thirty-three.

125
00:05:34,931 --> 00:05:37,622
Less money for the same
goods means lower prices.

126
00:05:38,002 --> 00:05:39,180
Here's the strange part.

127
00:05:39,560 --> 00:05:44,578
While banks were failing, people across
the country did exactly what Box Leo did.

128
00:05:44,958 --> 00:05:46,914
They pulled cash out and kept it at home.

129
00:05:47,294 --> 00:05:48,672
One shoebox looks safe.

130
00:05:49,052 --> 00:05:51,662
A whole country of shoeboxes
made the banks weaker.

131
00:05:52,392 --> 00:05:53,950
So Box Leo is the champion?

132
00:05:54,430 --> 00:05:55,428
Not so fast.

133
00:05:55,808 --> 00:05:57,350
A shoebox has its own traps.

134
00:05:57,730 --> 00:05:58,682
It earns nothing.

135
00:05:59,062 --> 00:05:59,882
It can burn.

136
00:06:00,262 --> 00:06:01,281
It can be stolen.

137
00:06:01,661 --> 00:06:06,321
And if you lose your job, you spend it,
bill by bill, until the box is empty.

138
00:06:06,701 --> 00:06:10,423
Box Leo still has to survive the
hardest moment of the whole game.

139
00:06:10,803 --> 00:06:12,012
Let's jump to the bottom.

140
00:06:12,742 --> 00:06:14,343
July eighth, nineteen thirty-two.

141
00:06:14,723 --> 00:06:16,942
The Dow closes at forty-one point two two.

142
00:06:17,322 --> 00:06:20,189
From the top, that's a fall
of about eighty-nine percent.

143
00:06:20,569 --> 00:06:24,752
Stock Leo's thousand dollars is now
about one hundred and eight dollars.

144
00:06:25,131 --> 00:06:27,088
That's price only, before dividends.

145
00:06:27,468 --> 00:06:30,784
Even with falling prices,
it buys only what about one

146
00:06:30,784 --> 00:06:34,025
hundred thirty-eight dollars
bought in nineteen twenty-nine.

147
00:06:34,405 --> 00:06:36,477
Dividends did help, but look at the gap.

148
00:06:36,857 --> 00:06:39,219
So how long until he's
back to one thousand?

149
00:06:39,699 --> 00:06:41,815
There are two answers,
and they're far apart.

150
00:06:42,195 --> 00:06:44,467
The Dow's price didn't
get back to its nineteen

151
00:06:44,467 --> 00:06:48,125
twenty-nine peak until
November nineteen fifty-four.

152
00:06:48,505 --> 00:06:52,820
Counting dividends and falling prices,
one well-known analysis puts

153
00:06:52,820 --> 00:06:57,022
the break-even point much earlier,
in late nineteen thirty-six.

154
00:06:57,402 --> 00:06:59,067
Still, that's seven years of waiting.

155
00:06:59,797 --> 00:07:02,283
Here's the scoreboard in
July nineteen thirty-two.

156
00:07:02,663 --> 00:07:06,261
Stock Leo: about one hundred and
eight dollars, on price alone.

157
00:07:06,641 --> 00:07:08,234
Bank Leo: a question mark.

158
00:07:08,614 --> 00:07:11,329
Maybe all of it, buying
just as much as Box Leo's.

159
00:07:11,709 --> 00:07:14,034
Maybe around eight hundred,
maybe still waiting.

160
00:07:14,414 --> 00:07:18,439
Box Leo: one thousand dollars that
buy like twelve hundred seventy.

161
00:07:18,819 --> 00:07:21,677
Every result on this board
belongs to that decade.

162
00:07:22,057 --> 00:07:23,757
A decade when prices were falling.

163
00:07:24,137 --> 00:07:26,489
That's what made a pile
of cash look so clever.

164
00:07:27,219 --> 00:07:28,410
But the game isn't over.

165
00:07:28,790 --> 00:07:30,743
It ends in March nineteen thirty-three.

166
00:07:31,123 --> 00:07:33,260
And March is when every door gets locked.

167
00:07:33,990 --> 00:07:35,391
Winter, nineteen thirty-three.

168
00:07:35,771 --> 00:07:37,445
Panic jumps from state to state.

169
00:07:37,825 --> 00:07:40,220
Visitors who come to
Washington for the new

170
00:07:40,220 --> 00:07:43,780
president's first day find
notes in their hotel rooms:

171
00:07:44,320 --> 00:07:46,838
checks from out-of-town
banks won't be accepted.

172
00:07:47,218 --> 00:07:50,542
By March fourth, every state
has declared a bank holiday.

173
00:07:50,922 --> 00:07:52,700
Two days later, the new president,

174
00:07:53,060 --> 00:07:56,217
Franklin Roosevelt, closes
every bank in the nation.

175
00:07:56,597 --> 00:07:58,475
Good banks and bad banks, all shut.

176
00:07:58,855 --> 00:08:00,512
Bank Leo stands at a locked door.

177
00:08:00,892 --> 00:08:04,180
For a few days, cash at home
is suddenly very useful,

178
00:08:04,500 --> 00:08:07,315
and Box Leo is the only
player holding any.

179
00:08:07,695 --> 00:08:11,300
When the holiday ends, about
four thousand banks never reopen.

180
00:08:11,680 --> 00:08:13,280
Then, in June nineteen thirty-three,

181
00:08:13,820 --> 00:08:16,844
a new law creates federal
deposit insurance.

182
00:08:17,224 --> 00:08:20,200
It starts on January first,
nineteen thirty-four,

183
00:08:20,800 --> 00:08:24,508
and covers up to two thousand five
hundred dollars per depositor.

184
00:08:25,238 --> 00:08:28,612
In nineteen thirty-four, only
nine insured banks suspended.

185
00:08:28,992 --> 00:08:31,437
The gamble door finally
gets a lock that works.

186
00:08:31,817 --> 00:08:34,060
So Box Leo wins nineteen twenty-nine:

187
00:08:34,740 --> 00:08:37,516
the same gain in buying
power, without the gamble.

188
00:08:37,896 --> 00:08:39,423
Now let's do something unfair.

189
00:08:39,803 --> 00:08:42,180
Let's take his winning
door and move it to today.

190
00:08:42,910 --> 00:08:43,806
Same shoebox.

191
00:08:44,186 --> 00:08:45,370
Same thousand dollars.

192
00:08:45,750 --> 00:08:47,819
Start in September twenty twenty-five.

193
00:08:48,199 --> 00:08:53,129
Twelve months later, U.S. consumer prices
are three point seven percent higher.

194
00:08:53,509 --> 00:08:57,453
That's the official number for the
year to September twenty twenty-six.

195
00:08:57,833 --> 00:09:00,836
So the thousand dollars
now buys what about nine

196
00:09:00,836 --> 00:09:03,769
hundred sixty-four dollars
bought a year earlier.

197
00:09:04,149 --> 00:09:05,156
Nothing was stolen.

198
00:09:05,536 --> 00:09:06,635
The bills are all there.

199
00:09:07,015 --> 00:09:08,068
They just buy less.

200
00:09:08,798 --> 00:09:10,813
Now put the two periods side by side.

201
00:09:11,193 --> 00:09:14,740
From September nineteen twenty-nine
to March nineteen thirty-three,

202
00:09:15,450 --> 00:09:17,685
prices fell about twenty-seven percent.

203
00:09:18,065 --> 00:09:21,480
From September twenty twenty-two
to September twenty twenty-six,

204
00:09:22,110 --> 00:09:24,870
prices rose about thirteen
and a half percent.

205
00:09:25,250 --> 00:09:26,050
Same box.

206
00:09:26,395 --> 00:09:27,321
Opposite result.

207
00:09:27,701 --> 00:09:32,121
In the early nineteen thirties, cash
gained power because prices were falling.

208
00:09:32,501 --> 00:09:37,470
In twenty twenty-six, prices are rising,
so the same math runs the other way.

209
00:09:37,850 --> 00:09:39,380
That's arithmetic, not a plan.

210
00:09:40,110 --> 00:09:41,476
The bank door changed too.

211
00:09:41,856 --> 00:09:44,292
That lock from nineteen
thirty-four is still there.

212
00:09:44,672 --> 00:09:48,520
Today, the FDIC says deposits
are insured to at least

213
00:09:48,520 --> 00:09:52,157
two hundred fifty thousand
dollars at each insured bank.

214
00:09:52,887 --> 00:09:54,086
So, which door was safe?

215
00:09:54,566 --> 00:09:57,942
In nineteen twenty-nine, the door
that looked smart hurt the most.

216
00:09:58,322 --> 00:10:00,473
The door everyone laughed
at came out on top.

217
00:10:00,853 --> 00:10:02,720
Not because a shoebox is clever,

218
00:10:02,960 --> 00:10:05,860
but because of one thing
outside all three doors:

219
00:10:06,240 --> 00:10:08,542
what prices were doing in that decade.

220
00:10:08,922 --> 00:10:10,633
That's the idea we started with.

221
00:10:11,013 --> 00:10:13,264
A safe place for money
isn't really a place.

222
00:10:13,644 --> 00:10:17,695
It depends on what prices are doing,
and on the rules around each door.

223
00:10:18,425 --> 00:10:20,451
Falling prices made a shoebox the winner.

224
00:10:20,831 --> 00:10:24,020
But history also has a
time when prices rose so

225
00:10:24,020 --> 00:10:27,362
fast that people's savings
melted in a single year.

226
00:10:27,742 --> 00:10:29,226
That's a game for another night.

227
00:10:29,606 --> 00:10:30,406
I'm Leo.

228
00:10:30,654 --> 00:10:31,580
Thanks for playing.
